1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,742
Tutor:

2
00:00:00,742 --> 00:00:05,531
So now I want you to discuss the lesson we’ve just been watching on the video

3
00:00:05,531 --> 00:00:10,843
and think about the ways in which origami can be a useful educational tool.

4
00:00:10,843 --> 00:00:14,570
Can you all work with the person sitting next to you …

5
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:14,620
Seb:

6
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:15,624
I had no idea that such a simple thing like folding squares of paper

7
00:00:15,624 --> 00:00:16,607
to make the shape of something like a bird could be such an amazing

8
00:00:16,607 --> 00:00:16,677
tool.

9
00:00:16,677 --> 00:00:17,450
It’s made me see origami in a whole new light.

10
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:17,798
Lia:

11
00:00:17,798 --> 00:00:18,495
I know.

12
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:23,373
It was interesting to see the educational skills the children were developing by doing

13
00:00:23,373 --> 00:00:23,721
origami.

14
00:00:23,721 --> 00:00:28,599
On the video you could see them really listening hard to make sure they

15
00:00:28,599 --> 00:00:32,780
did all the steps in the right order to make the bird.

16
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:33,199
Seb:

17
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,454
That’s right.

18
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:40,315
In this lesson they were working individually but it would also be interesting to

19
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:46,175
see if the children could work out how to make something simple without being

20
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:47,431
given any direction.

21
00:00:47,431 --> 00:00:50,780
That would help with building teamwork as well.

22
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:51,175
Lia:

23
00:00:51,175 --> 00:00:51,570
Yes,

24
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:53,939
but much more of a challenge.

25
00:00:53,939 --> 00:00:59,466
One thing that really stood out for me was that the children were all

26
00:00:59,466 --> 00:01:02,230
having fun while being taught something new.

27
00:01:02,230 --> 00:01:02,426
Seb:

28
00:01:02,426 --> 00:01:04,774
Which is a key aim of any lesson with this age group.

29
00:01:04,774 --> 00:01:07,513
And although these kids had no problems with folding the paper, with younger children

30
00:01:07,513 --> 00:01:09,470
you could do origami to help practise fine motor skills.

31
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:09,855
Lia:

32
00:01:09,855 --> 00:01:10,241
Absolutely.

33
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:14,866
Shall we talk about the individual children we saw on the video?

34
00:01:14,866 --> 00:01:18,720
I wrote all their names down and took some notes.

35
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:19,836
Seb:

36
00:01:19,836 --> 00:01:24,300
Yes, I did too.

37
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:25,342
Lia:

38
00:01:25,342 --> 00:01:27,428
OK, good.

39
00:01:27,428 --> 00:01:32,640
Let’s start with Sid.

40
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,154
Seb:

41
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:40,356
He was interesting because before they started doing the origami, he was being quite

42
00:01:40,356 --> 00:01:40,870
disruptive.

43
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:41,324
Lia:

44
00:01:41,324 --> 00:01:41,778
Yes.

45
00:01:41,778 --> 00:01:48,588
He really benefited from having to use his hands – it helped him to settle down

46
00:01:48,588 --> 00:01:49,950
and start concentrating.

47
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:50,240
Seb:

48
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:51,687
Yes, I noticed that too.

49
00:01:51,687 --> 00:01:52,556
What about Jack?

50
00:01:52,556 --> 00:01:56,030
I noticed he seemed to want to work things out for himself.

51
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:56,398
Lia:

52
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:56,766
Mmm.

53
00:01:56,766 --> 00:02:01,915
You could see him trying out different things rather than asking the teacher for

54
00:02:01,915 --> 00:02:02,283
help.

55
00:02:02,283 --> 00:02:04,490
What did you make of Naomi?

56
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:04,866
Seb:

57
00:02:04,866 --> 00:02:10,128
She seemed to be losing interest at one point but then she decided she

58
00:02:10,128 --> 00:02:15,390
wanted her mouse to be the best and that motivated her to try harder.

59
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:15,595
Lia:

60
00:02:15,595 --> 00:02:17,850
She didn’t seem satisfied with hers in the end, though.

61
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,520
Seb:

62
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:27,190
No.

63
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:27,830
Lia:

64
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:31,033
Anya was such a star.

65
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:40,000
She listened so carefully and then produced the perfect bird with very little effort.

66
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:40,273
Seb:

67
00:02:40,273 --> 00:02:44,100
Mmm – I think the teacher could have increased the level of difficulty for her.

68
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:44,386
Lia:

69
00:02:44,386 --> 00:02:44,671
Maybe.

70
00:02:44,671 --> 00:02:48,100
I think it was the first time Zara had come across origami.

71
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,069
Seb:

72
00:02:49,069 --> 00:03:01,670
She looked as if she didn’t really get what was going on.

73
00:03:01,670 --> 00:03:01,965
Lia:

74
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:04,920
She seemed unsure about what she was supposed to do,

75
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:08,170
but in the end hers didn’t turn out too badly.

76
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:08,663
Seb:

77
00:03:08,663 --> 00:03:09,156
Yeah.

78
00:03:09,156 --> 00:03:14,580
I’m sure it was a positive learning experience for her.

79
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:14,692
Lia:

80
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:16,257
I think one reason why the origami activity worked so well in this class

81
00:03:16,257 --> 00:03:17,040
was that the teacher was well prepared.

82
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:17,124
Seb:

83
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:17,208
Right.

84
00:03:17,208 --> 00:03:18,386
I think it would have taken me ages to prepare examples, showing each of

85
00:03:18,386 --> 00:03:18,974
the steps involved in making the bird.

86
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:19,563
But that was a really good idea.

87
00:03:19,563 --> 00:03:20,320
The children could see what they were aiming for –

88
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:21,498
and much better for them to be able to hold something, rather than just

89
00:03:21,498 --> 00:03:21,750
looking at pictures.

90
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:22,430
Lia:

91
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:29,230
Mmm – those physical examples supported her verbal explanations really well.

92
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:29,505
Seb:

93
00:03:29,505 --> 00:03:32,252
It’s strange that origami isn’t used more widely.

94
00:03:32,252 --> 00:03:33,900
Why do you think that is?

95
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:34,162
Lia:

96
00:03:34,162 --> 00:03:38,086
Well, teachers may just feel it’s not that appealing to children who are used

97
00:03:38,086 --> 00:03:39,917
to doing everything on computers, especially boys.

98
00:03:39,917 --> 00:03:42,010
Even if they’re aware of the benefits.

99
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:42,341
Seb:

100
00:03:42,341 --> 00:03:43,994
Oh, I don’t know.

101
00:03:43,994 --> 00:03:46,971
It’s no different to any other craft activity.

102
00:03:46,971 --> 00:03:50,940
I bet it’s because so many teachers are clumsy like me.

103
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:51,492
Lia:

104
00:03:51,492 --> 00:03:59,228
That’s true – too much effort required if you’re not good with your

105
00:03:59,228 --> 00:03:59,780
hands.

106
00:03:59,780 --> 00:03:59,855
Seb:

107
00:03:59,855 --> 00:04:00,900
Well, anyway, I think we should try it out in our maths teaching practice

108
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:01,124
with Year 3.

109
00:04:01,124 --> 00:04:02,244
I can see using origami is a really engaging way of reinforcing children’s knowledge

110
00:04:02,244 --> 00:04:02,991
of geometric shapes, like they were doing in the video,

111
00:04:02,991 --> 00:04:03,887
but I think it would also work really well for presenting fractions,

112
00:04:03,887 --> 00:04:04,260
which is coming up soon.

113
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:04,411
Lia:

114
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:06,519
Good idea – that’s something most of the kids in that class might struggle

115
00:04:06,519 --> 00:04:06,670
with.

116
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:08,025
Origami would also be good practice for using symmetry –

117
00:04:08,025 --> 00:04:09,230
but I think they did that last term.

118
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:09,812
Seb:

119
00:04:09,812 --> 00:04:15,636
OK – well let’s try and get some ideas together

120
00:04:15,636 --> 00:04:19,130
and plan the lesson next week.

121
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:25,287
Tutor:

122
00:04:25,287 --> 00:05:02,230
OK, if you could all stop …
